						<div class='article-twenty-twenty-style-content mb-5 '><p>I was half dead in the Jacksonville airport. I was exhausted and they had a group of middle schoolers up in the bleachers singing Christmas carols. I was keeping my head down. I had my sunglasses on, and I wasn’t trying to cause trouble. Honest to God, I was mostly thinking about the parking garage. I was thinking about the fact that I had not operated a motor vehicle in about seven years and I had the fear deep in me about getting the car out of that parking garage.</p>
<p>I was alone driving from the East Coast of Florida to Joshua Tree, in California. That was a normal thing to do after a breakup. My boyfriend had just dumped me. I wasn’t surprised. We kept having the same fight. But after I’d landed, successfully made it out of the airport parking garage, and driven about sixty miles, I pulled into a gas station in Lake City and started pacing in the light rain. The breakup was inconvenient because we had planned to take the trip together. It would have been romantic. Camping in a minor national park. Eating barbecue on the side of a county highway. At the very least, it would have been useful to have him around. My ex-boyfriend had opinions about women operating heavy machinery, and he could’ve driven the car.</p>
<p>In the gas station I drank a Celsius. Then I ate a turkey sandwich, with sweet pickles and mayonnaise. I chugged half a bottle of water. I took off my pants and changed into a pair of pink shorts, made of some kind of canvas material. And I got back in the car. I wasn’t going to back out. I was writing a piece about teenage girls in America, driving across the country to interview them, and I had already found the girls I would talk to. Almost all of them were the children of friends of friends, because when you suggest to a complete stranger that you want to hang out with their teen daughter and just chat, people assume things. I explained that I wanted to ask the girls what they liked to do, where they hung out. This seemed like a good enough place to start. I wanted to see how their lives mapped onto my own experiences of being a teenager, to see what, if anything, had changed.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>When I arrived in Pensacola the sun was setting. It was around four thirty in the afternoon. The sky was a milky blue. The same color as the water. It had been five hours in the car and the roads were empty. As I exited the bridge, the beach came into focus. A sign, done up in little light bulbs: <span class="small_caps">turn right pensacola beach scenic drive east along gulf of america!</span> On the top is a marlin, lit up in green, blue, yellow, and red. Tiki bars and seafood restaurants. A Hooters and an Elks Lodge.</p>
<p>I know that my hotel is called Spring Break For Ever. That in March and April, co-eds will drive south and east, in their neon-colored bathing suits, and vomit into wicker trash cans next to the rainfall shower.</p>
<p>But it was not like this in December. There was almost no one checked into the hotel, and I was upgraded to a deluxe king suite. I was here to meet Al. We were going to meet at a Barnes &amp; Noble, in a strip mall.</p>
<p>Al’s mom is a goth lady in her mid-thirties. She’s a hairdresser, and her hair is an electric aquamarine color. Her name is Sidni, originally from Memphis. But she’s lived in Pensacola since high school. ‘Have you ever been,’ Sidni asks me, ‘this low before?’ Referring to the South. I answer: Not Really. In the early 2000s, Sidni tells me, she went to grindcore shows, here in Pensacola. She had Al when she was in her twenties, in 2010. My first interaction with Al is over text. I ask Al: do you have some time to talk? Al responds: I’m available to call whenever you’d like! (●´ϖ`●), then I remember asking her, What’s it like being a teen in Pensacola! ‘Uhhhhhh,’ she responded.</p>
<p>Al is open with me in a way that catches me off guard. There is no warm-up period. She is willing to talk to me about nearly everything, excited that someone is paying attention to her and wants to hear her theories. Al is funny, bright, and talks at a million miles a minute. She has stuff she wants to show me. Her pin collection: A Daria pin, pins of anime characters. Her Sonic the Hedgehog TekDek (she says she’s pretty good at doing tricks but <em>not that good</em>). She hunches over the table we’re sitting at in Starbucks and squints, taking her Sonic the Hedgehog TekDek and flipping it over with two of her fingers. Al has shoulder-length brown hair, with the ends dyed bleach blonde. She’s wearing a blue Hello Kitty graphic T-shirt and a blue sweatshirt. She has on a mini skirt and cut-out tights and a raccoon tail. She rolls her eyes a lot. She has a deep voice. She loves to talk about <em>Homestuck</em>, her favorite web comic. She says <em>dude</em> a lot. We split a brownie and a croissant, and I get Al a green tea, to which she adds a pack of sugar and also two coffee creamers. Every so often though, I catch something in her eye. Maybe a moment of mistrust. She turns around and looks at her mom’s partner, who is sitting at a laptop at a table near us. He’s not listening.</p>
<p>Al is extremely opinionated. ‘I’m not super far liberal and I’m not super far right,’ she says, ‘I think everyone is stupid, but I hate Donald Trump.’ She likes the bands Car Seat Headrest, Hole, and Pavement. She has thousands of liked songs saved on Spotify. She loves <em>Scott Pilgrim vs. the World</em> and <em>Full Metal Alchemist</em>. She tells me that she is taking a vow of silence at school right now because she watched <em>Little Miss Sunshine</em> and decided she was exactly the same as the Paul Dano character. Also because ‘I just wasn’t getting my crap done. It was because I was talking too much. You have got to cut down on that. My friends would be like that doesn’t make sense? And I was like ummmmm . . . it’s because you DON’T GET IT!’ Then she adds: ‘I feel like a lot of my friends are . . . how do I say this . . . Very Dull Sometimes. They don’t think a lot. They’re very immature. I mean, we’re fourteen-year-olds, I get it . . . Have you read Nietzsche before?’ I tell her I have, a little bit, in college. She says: ‘I thought it was a common thing to know who Nietzsche was until recently.’</p>
<p>In school, Al has just written a paper about the Civil War. ‘Why it’s so important that people are properly educated on the Civil War.’ Al says writing isn’t really her thing, but she loves drawing, and shows me some drawings she’s made on an iPad of her original characters. I ask Al if she wants to be an artist. She tells me it’s only a hobby. ‘I want to, like, be a voice actor or something,’ she says, ‘I think I could do that. But voice acting doesn’t pay well. Unless you’re famous. I’m not famous. I could be famous. I doubt it would be for the right reason though. It would be, like, for throwing coffee at some douche celebrity or something.’</p>
<p>Al doesn’t really identify as anything. She says she’s pretty gender fluid. ‘I used to present as more masculine. I don’t anymore. It changes for me. Sometimes I’m feeling more feminine, sometimes I’m feeling more masculine.’ She shows me a photo of herself around that time, in middle school, where she has a short, bleach-blonde haircut. She looks like Kurt Cobain. She tells me doesn’t really care what pronouns people use for her. ‘Just not they/them,’ she laughs. I ask her if she can tell me anything else about middle school, which for her was right around the time Covid lockdowns ended. ‘Honestly,’ she says, ‘I don’t remember anything about Covid.’</p>
<p>Al is dating a girl right now, she thinks she could maybe fall in love with her. But she’s not sure. She doesn’t know if she will fall in love. ‘Love feels really factory made to me,’ she says. I think to myself: well, yeah. I ask her: Do you think you’re someone who is extremely aware of how old you are and where you are in your life? She says: ‘Yeah. I think I come off as someone who thinks they’re better than everybody else. But I promise you I’m not.’</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The early 2010s. Schenectady; an unremarkable place to grow up. The site of the first ever General Electric plant. I was from a nice town next to the city. A white colonial with cranberry shutters. A quiet suburban street with big trees. A rhododendron bush. A large public high school. I took a vow in the ninth grade that I would never wear pants; that I would never listen to music by men. It wasn’t so much that I didn’t have many friends (I didn’t have many friends) but that I was actively despised. Like Al, I talked too much, I did not get my shit done. I sat alone at lunch. Not even in the lunchroom. In a vestibule in the front of the school, across from a security attendant. I would listen to my iPod. I would eat a plain turkey sandwich. I would think to myself: I am so interesting. I would think to myself: I hate everyone in the whole world.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Biloxi is a place I always remembered because I like the name. Mobile is a place I’ve always pronounced the wrong way. I would say <em>MoebIsle</em>. You’re supposed to pronounce it like this: MoeBeel. Someone tells me Biloxi is the Vegas of the Gulf Coast. Cecily tells me Mobile is a pretty weird place to grow up. But she’s not quite from Mobile. She’s from Spanish Fort.</p>
<p>Cecily is seventeen years old. Three years older than Al, at an age where three years feels like twenty. She is the girlfriend of my friend’s much younger little brother. Spanish Fort, she tells me, has only really suburbanized in the past decade or so. It was very Rural before. Cecily doesn’t live there anymore. Things are bad with her parents. Her father kicked her out. Cecily’s mother is a self-proclaimed witch and, Cecily tells me, has gotten arrested for taking all her clothes off in the street.</p>
<p>Cecily goes to a prestigious public art high school in Birmingham, Alabama. This is where she lives. The school has dorms, for boarders, since it covers the whole state of Alabama, not just Birmingham and its suburbs. Cecily wants to be a famous writer. I asked her to write her life story.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><span class="small_caps">cecily’s autobiography</span><br />
By Cecily Andromeda Turner</p>
<blockquote>
<p style="text-align: left;">I am a young essayist born in the Holy Gardens of Swampy South Alabama. Well, actually, I was born in Largo, Florida, but I was reborn whilst smoking a cigarette in eighth grade amid the swamp behind my Grandma Kay’s Easter-yellow antebellum commune house. I’ve been an enigma to myself since that very day when I realized that if I told everyone around me everything that was on my mind constantly, they’d somehow respect me more, even if the thing that was on my mind was the bus driver that drove me home every day in elementary school (I was the first one on, last one off, so to me, it felt like father and daughter, or boss and assistant, or Keith Carradine and Brooke Shields in <em>Pretty Baby</em> 1978). When the bus driver retired, I was in fourth grade, and I prematurely masturbated thinking of his pleather, ripped up, foam seat; it was merely an ancient, ritualistic commemoration of the dearest elder who hadever shepherded me. It was a pure thing.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Now I go to a fine arts high school in the downtown of an Alabamian cityscape because of my expertise in being shepherded and pure and every Southern teenage girl in my school wants to audition for me because they think I’m always thinking of them when I write, which I am. In the summers and winters, I live with my boyfriend’s fabulous . . . altruistic . . . Phoenician cashmere sort of family, that’s the most accurate way to describe the beauty and loveliness of this family, yes, and it’s been a year since I last saw my blood family but they do get in touch once a month to send me a photo of my mangy dog, Gunner, because they know he’s the only one who ever held me, down there by the bay. Their South-Alabamian love was never meant for me, and that’s okay. I mean, how could a lion and a scorpion bond with a swamp swan? Gin Rummy?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The way Cecily talks is not so different from the way she writes. Which is to say she talks like her hair is on fire. Here is what Cecily said to me, when I asked her to describe herself:</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><span class="small_caps">as told by cecily</span><br />
Told to Sophie Frances Kemp by Cecily Andromeda Turner</p>
<blockquote>
<p style="text-align: left;">I have a strong sense of self love. I always thought it was confidence or self-esteem, but now I’m not sure. I’m very optimistic. I’m very spiritual. I believe in prayer and karma. I feel very otherworldly. I’ve always felt really different. I would either feel really well liked or really hated. I’ve had times in my life where I have experienced every single type of being liked socially. I smoked weed for the first time when I was thirteen because my dad gave it to me. There was another time where I accidentally took a 1000mg edible and got HPPD. I felt completely depersonalized. I had spiritual psychosis when I was twelve. Now I can’t do any drugs. All of this happened while I was taking care of my sister with autism and sleeping on an air mattress and I became obsessed with going to Bosnia and moving to Moldova and starting a new life. I was posting all of this on Instagram and getting all of this response and positive feedback. People thought I was doing, like, an art experiment but I was just feeling crazy. People thought I was a child artist but I was living in my dad’s two-bedroom apartment in the middle-of-nowhere Mobile. But then I suddenly became so confident. I felt like I had become fully realized. Like a Hindu. I felt like I didn’t have a body. I felt like I was living in an asteroid. I felt amazing but also like I was going to die. But now I feel so much more normal. I realized there was so much inside of myself if I chased my artistic aspirations. I thought I had to use what I had. It made me want to be a writer.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">I was eleven during Covid. I was already so disconnected from the real world because after Covid my dad was like: you’re already getting bullied let’s have you leave school. So I didn’t really go to middle school. Three months in seventh grade. I was more or less completely out of school. I had all this free time to go on the internet. I had this internet persona as a political TikToker but I had a fifth-grade education. I said I was an anarcho-communist even though I did not know what that meant. I was still in that house and everyone else was so much more In Real Life than me. They were all Trump supporters and didn’t wear masks. I had no idea what was going on with Covid except via radical forty-year-olds on TikTok. In 2020 I only left the house seven times in the whole year. This was around the time I got my period. I got my period in fifth grade. The day I got my first period was the day Jeffrey Epstein died.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">The first televised catastrophe I remember is Sandy Hook. I was obsessed with school shooters when I was like eleven. I was born in 2008. People my age don’t feel like 9/11 is a big deal. People are obsessed with Jeffrey Epstein. The worst thing that could happen is an elementary school shooting. Everything I write ends with childlike optimism. I write so much about children because I feel so interested in innocence and the way children think about things. I’ve always felt so much more curious and naive than other people.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p></blockquote>
<p>Cecily believes that people are good until proven otherwise. But as much as Cecily loves the South, and loves Alabama, she wants to leave.</p>
<p>This is a crucial moment for a teenage girl. You realize you have got to get out, and that you’re obsessed with this about yourself. You romanticize your own unhappiness. You feel different, alienated, and drunk on the promise of your own future. I remember feeling like that too.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>On the road, I keep thinking about walking into my childhood bedroom. But I am inside my adult body, and it’s not really my bedroom. It is a set in the black box of a theatre and teenage me is sitting on my bed. I want to tell her about what happens when you get older. How it is better but also worse. I start to tell her about my breakup. That I had fallen in love with someone who would make me feel like either a dancing circus monkey or a blow-up anime sex doll. There are so many things that will happen to you that you won’t believe! I’d tell her. You will get wiser, but it will not get easier. But she doesn’t care, she’s not listening.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In Greenville, I crossed the Mississippi River. This is where, overlooking the water from my rental car, I thought about the American Girl’s pupal stage. About my pupal stage. The moment when a girl becomes an extraordinary machine. A vessel for a new kind of attention. When a girl grows up just enough and all eyes are on her. When all those changes were happening to my body, I barely thought about it. I had woken up with hips, a nice ass, grown lanky. I looked like my mother, my beautiful mother.</p>
<p>What I desired was to get fucked by basically any boy or man. What I desired was: to get fucked by a group of gorgeous blonde volleyball player girls. Or one of the out high school lesbians (there were four of them) who wore T-shirts that said: <span class="small_caps">i am a vaginatarian<span class="small_caps">!</span></span> I wanted to drink liquor at a party at one of the mansions next to the park named after a famous Olympic wrestler.</p>
<p>But instead, I sat in front of the family computer sexting men from Chatroulette. It was less about getting off and more about being someone else. Heeyyyyy. I’d say. im Molly. im 20 years old and i live in Williamsburg and go to shows at 285 Kent. They would say: are u wet for me sexy girl !!!!!! i live Romania. I’d say: i go 2 NYU and have green hair. Afterwards I’d hump a pillow and think about what it would be like either to hold hands with a boy who knew what pitchfork.com was or what it would be like to be a giant bug. I could not figure out how to achieve orgasm. I threw up from menstruating. I had to go to an audiologist because I thought I was going deaf but instead was diagnosed with a vague neurological condition frequently found in individuals on the Autism Spectrum. To correct my chronic nosebleeds, a different doctor stuck a lit match of topical silver nitrate so far up my nasal cavity, I felt like perhaps it was touching my brain. I never got breasts. Just shapely areolae. I shaved different parts of my head. I voluntarily got a bowl cut. I went to prom without a date. My father bought me my corsage. This was when ‘Work’ by Iggy Azalea was all the rage. The only thing I was above average at athletically was running fast, so I ran laps around the school track listening to ‘Work’ and saying to myself: I Pledge Allegiance to the Struggle. I viewed myself as crazy, untouchable, because this was more or less true.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>Zola is fifteen years old. She has a buzzcut, she has a ton of piercings, both on her ears and on her face (she tells me she did most of them herself, including her septum). I pick Zola up from school. She’s changed out of her uniform to meet me, and is now wearing enormously baggy jeans, a T-shirt with the Venus de Milo, a hand-crocheted black and white striped pussy hat, Converse hi-tops that she’s embroidered, and a necklace with two charms: a Star of David and two girl signs linked. We are in city in Texas. I have chosen to stop here because my friend Lucy lives here, and she put me in touch with Zola’s dad.</p>
<p>‘I’ve already had a few classic teenage experiences,’ says Zola. Then she tells me three classic teenage things that teenagers do to defy their parents that are so funny and so ordinary. My reaction to this surprises me. I feel the urge to say to her: be careful Zola.</p>
<p>We are in my car, driving through the city’s highway system, its can of worms. I am terrified to be driving her, but I do not tell her this. We are headed to the mall.</p>
<p>Zola tells me that she’s gay within two minutes of our drive. I tell her I am sort of gay. ‘I always have a boyfriend,’ I say, ‘but I HAVE dated women before.’ Zola laughs. At the mall, we will meet her friend. Zola’s parents liked the idea of having her chat with me alongside a friend.</p>
<p>The girls are on a mission today: to shop. Our itinerary is to go to Zumiez, Hot Topic, and the consumer electronics store FYE. Zola says that she and Jade will go to Spencer’s, but I’m not invited. Spencer’s is a glorified vibrator store. That’s none of my business.</p>
<p>Her friend is wearing bedazzled low-rise jeans, DC sneakers, a white fur hood that she will leave up the entirety of our time together because she thinks her hair looks bad, and has a BOOBIES lanyard coming out of the pockets of one of her pants. Just like every gay girl I knew in Schenectady, circa 2011. The only difference is that now teen gay girls also own a vape.</p>
<p>In Hot Topic, the girls look at Hello Kitty thongs and Stranger Things silk bonnets. A clean version of a Taylor Swift song plays and Zola tells me she is madly in love with her new girlfriend. She lives,’ says Zola, ‘sixteen hours away in Chicago.’</p>
<p>At Barnes &amp; Noble, Zola wants a chocolate cake pop and a latte and her friend wants a black coffee with six pumps of sugar-free sugar and almond milk.</p>
<p>‘When you’ve been in love,’ I ask, ‘what was it like?’ Zola’s last relationship ended badly, but she doesn’t regret it. ‘When you have so much love for somebody, it’s hard because it feels like your heart is breaking.’ I notice that the girls are holding hands, not in a way that is romantic, but in a way that is like: You’re okay.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>I realised that I could not stop asking the girls about love and if they thought it was real.</p>
<p>Unlike them, I was never in love with anyone as a teenage girl. But I was in love with the idea of being in love.</p>
<p>I didn’t actually fall in love for the first time until I moved to Paris. I knew it was love because I felt different. I wanted to tell this to the girls. Not any of the details. But the part where it took me a long time. I wanted to tell them that I didn’t fall in love until I was nearly twenty-one, but that over the course of my twenties I would fall in love four other times. That each time it happened I became a different person, that it was scary, that it wasn’t always good, that sometimes I still felt exactly the same as I had done as a teenager. And that none of that made me special.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>In New Mexico Lily and Savannah spoke to me like they were in detention and I was forcing them to be there. These were teen girls par excellence. These were teen girls from a movie about teen girls.</p>
<p>Lily and Savannah are both freshmen in high school. They’re both very beautiful. Lily has dyed blonde hair (she tells me she is a natural blonde, she just has highlights). She has big eyes in a Sissy Spacek way. She speaks with a gravely alto, deeper than you’d think. Savannah is tall and built like a ballerina, with long dark hair. They are both wearing sweatpants. They both have lash extensions and acrylic nails. When Savannah and Lily talk to me, they look anywhere that isn’t my face.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>I have some experience with girls like this. Suburban girls who ice-skated and did gymnastics and went to cheer camp and Christian camp and rode horses. Girls with lake houses on Lake George, where they would water-ski. Girls who went to Disney in Orlando for winter break. Sleepover parties where we would all sleep on the floor in the TV room, swaddled in Barbie comforters on blow-up beds atop sand-colored wall-to-wall carpeting. I would call my mom on the phone and ask if I had to stay.</p>
<p>The main places Lily and Savannah hang out that aren’t ‘walking around’ are an indoor trampoline park and a second-rate amusement park. They skip class sometimes. But when they do, they don’t even leave campus. They just ‘chill in a car’ for an hour then go back to class. In the car, they hang out with their boyfriends. They play a lot of Spanish language music. Or, in Lily’s words ‘Spanish music, hoodie and ghetto rap’ which she ‘doesn’t mind’. The girls add: ‘We don’t speak Spanish.’</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>They both had their first ‘serious relationship’ when they were in sixth grade. When I ask Lily about this, what it felt like to date someone for the first time, she says: ‘Ew, gross.’ But then she elaborates, ‘It was maybe like the end of sixth grade. That was when I had my first real boyfriend, we dated for a year.’</p>
<p>When Lily looks back at her past relationships, she says ‘Ew, gross,’ again, then says that she finds people she used to date ‘disgusting’. I tell her that I understand what she means. What they want for the future is someone they can build something with, a man who has the capacity to be not only a partner but also a close friend.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>At school, I could never figure out girls like Savannah and Lily. The girls who went to house parties. Who were fluent socially. Who did not say every single thing that popped into their head. It felt like these teenagers were better at being girls than me. For them, it seemed so easy. But now I know that none of us felt normal.</p>
<p>The only advantage girls like Lily and Savannah, and the girls who drove around in their parents’ BMWs with boys who played lacrosse, had on me is that they learned to blend in. They struggled in a way that I didn’t understand at sixteen, but I understand now, at thirty.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p style="text-align: center;">–</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>My last stretch of the drive was a one-hundred-mile desert corridor on California State Route 62. On this stretch of highway, there are no gas stations, no small towns to stop in for lunch. There is limited cell phone service. It was a sunny day with perfect visibility. I had started to weep and the mountains went in and out of focus. Why was I crying? I was crying because I was physically exhausted. I was crying because I was hungry, because I wanted to eat this one specific sandwich, because I had developed sciatica in my ass from sitting in a stress position while driving, and I wanted to get out of that car. I wanted to sleep in the same bed for more than two nights. I wanted to wear a nice outfit. I was crying because of my ex-boyfriend. I realized I was no longer sad about our breakup, I was just angry. I was crying because talking to the girls had reminded me what it was like to be fifteen, and I was frustrated because remembering this feeling had not fixed me. It had not reignited my belief in love. This shouldn’t have been a surprise: your child self lives in the past and you have no right to go back there.</p>
<p>But the drive had also reminded me of what I had wanted, back when I was fifteen: to become a writer, to have someone light a cigarette for me in front of a bar, to have someone kiss me on the mouth. I remembered what it felt like to have a crush and realize it was making me horny.</p>
<p>The girls I met want a lot of the same things I wanted. They want a lot of the things you might expect a teenage girl to want. It’s the same old story. It’s what you see in the movies.</p>
<p>Girls want true love. They want a boyfriend. They want a girlfriend. They want to drink alcohol that tastes like candy. They want to sing along to the words. They want to buy a vape. They want to get piercings at the mall. They want to piss off their parents. They want to feel heartbroken, because they think that’s a very adult thing to do. But they want to be understood and they really really really want someone to see them. And see that they want so many things that you might never expect. They want people to talk to them like they are smart. They are figuring it all out for the first time. They will realize that these years they are living through are the years where they are becoming themselves, that is a beautiful and rare thing, and they will remember these years with contempt but also with awe.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
